WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.813 --> 00:00:04.146
(cheerful techno music)

00:00:09.945 --> 00:00:12.862
(crowd chattering)

00:00:19.000 --> 00:00:21.470
- So, my name is Nadya
Bair and I'm a post doc

00:00:21.470 --> 00:00:24.320
in Digital Humanities
and American Studies.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:26.580
So my project is called
The Decisive Network,

00:00:26.580 --> 00:00:28.960
Magnum Photos and the
Postwar Image Market.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:31.460
And it's related to my book
manuscript which is a history

00:00:31.460 --> 00:00:33.280
of the photo agency Magnum.

00:00:33.280 --> 00:00:35.430
So something that I was
really interested in

00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:37.200
is Magnum is really known for a couple

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:40.590
of its famous photographers
and their iconic images

00:00:40.590 --> 00:00:42.690
that they produced, and what
I was interested in doing

00:00:42.690 --> 00:00:43.630
is a couple of things.

00:00:43.630 --> 00:00:46.580
First, based on the archival
research that I did,

00:00:46.580 --> 00:00:49.220
showcasing the labor of other staff

00:00:49.220 --> 00:00:51.680
who helped the photographers become famous

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:53.260
and who helped them produce their works.

00:00:53.260 --> 00:00:57.180
So the largest view here is
dedicated to really bringing

00:00:57.180 --> 00:01:00.770
to life people like
secretaries and editors.

00:01:00.770 --> 00:01:02.860
People who worked behind the scenes

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:06.250
on editing Magnum films and
distributing Magnum Films.

00:01:06.250 --> 00:01:09.560
Another part of the project
that I'm interested in doing

00:01:09.560 --> 00:01:12.690
is showing how agents, sales agents,

00:01:12.690 --> 00:01:14.890
sold Magnum's pictures around the world.

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:19.080
So, the idea is to have a map of the world

00:01:19.080 --> 00:01:21.750
showing how post, and after World War 2,

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:25.330
magazine markets emerged
and how Magnum was servicing

00:01:25.330 --> 00:01:27.320
those markets with its pictures.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:29.690
And then the third part
is to pick a couple

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:33.610
of iconic photo stories and to show

00:01:33.610 --> 00:01:35.600
how they also circulated around the world

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:37.210
and the intervention there is,

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:38.540
especially in the United States,

00:01:38.540 --> 00:01:41.310
we often think about
photo journalism history

00:01:41.310 --> 00:01:43.100
as being about Life magazine,

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:46.880
and the goal here is to show
that these photographers

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:49.710
were selling to a whole variety of markets

00:01:49.710 --> 00:01:51.140
and that they were actually influencing

00:01:51.140 --> 00:01:54.080
global visual culture and not
just American visual culture

00:01:54.080 --> 00:01:55.980
with the pictures they were producing.

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:59.100
It's been really great to collaborate

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.510
with a digital humanities lab at Yale,

00:02:01.510 --> 00:02:04.010
particularly because I found that a lot

00:02:04.010 --> 00:02:07.550
of the pre-existing software
on network visualization

00:02:07.550 --> 00:02:10.940
sort of all looked the same
and put you into a mold

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:13.470
of having to represent
networks in a particular kind

00:02:13.470 --> 00:02:17.110
of 21st century way, and
what I really wanted to do

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:20.230
was to represent these
networks and these ideas

00:02:20.230 --> 00:02:22.750
in the style of postwar visual cultures.

00:02:22.750 --> 00:02:27.020
So, the designer Monica Ong Reed,

00:02:27.020 --> 00:02:30.400
I showed her some of
the publicity materials

00:02:30.400 --> 00:02:32.380
that Magnum was creating about itself

00:02:32.380 --> 00:02:34.360
and so what we've done is we've come up,

00:02:34.360 --> 00:02:37.030
she's come up with ways
to model the presentation

00:02:37.030 --> 00:02:40.180
of this information on that
mid-century visual culture

00:02:40.180 --> 00:02:42.630
and that's really exciting to me.

00:02:42.630 --> 00:02:47.610
- The basis for the project
is a tool to explore

00:02:47.610 --> 00:02:49.840
the testimonies of Holocaust survivors,

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:54.530
witnesses and bystanders
from 3 different collections,

00:02:54.530 --> 00:02:56.350
the Fortunoff video archive, which has,

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:59.530
is a collection of 4,500 testimonies,

00:02:59.530 --> 00:03:01.280
the USC Shoah Foundation,

00:03:01.280 --> 00:03:03.950
and the US Holocaust Memorial Museum.

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:05.530
Both the USC Shoah Foundation and

00:03:05.530 --> 00:03:08.190
the US Holocaust Memorial
Museum have contributed

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:11.720
over 1,000 testimony transcripts
each to this project.

00:03:11.720 --> 00:03:14.037
In our collection, combined with that,

00:03:14.037 --> 00:03:17.670
comes to about 3,000
testimony transcripts.

00:03:17.670 --> 00:03:21.480
The anthology is very much a project

00:03:21.480 --> 00:03:22.990
that is similar to others,
sort of traditional,

00:03:22.990 --> 00:03:27.580
historical work in which
a body of text is explored

00:03:27.580 --> 00:03:30.850
and similar experiences
are brought together

00:03:30.850 --> 00:03:33.760
from those texts to explore more,

00:03:33.760 --> 00:03:38.303
let's say, common experiences
that survivors may have met

00:03:39.875 --> 00:03:44.875
in the camps or prior to
or in the post war period.

00:03:46.610 --> 00:03:51.060
The anthology however, uses sophisticated

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:56.060
data text mining tools to
explore this very large amount

00:03:56.870 --> 00:04:00.010
of data in order to pull those
common experiences together.

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:03.930
The other aspect of this
project is a search tool

00:04:03.930 --> 00:04:06.220
which allows researchers and educators

00:04:06.220 --> 00:04:10.610
to search across all of these
3,000 testimony transcripts

00:04:10.610 --> 00:04:13.750
in sophisticated ways to locate subjects

00:04:13.750 --> 00:04:15.770
related to their research topics.

00:04:15.770 --> 00:04:17.530
So there's really two
sides to the project,

00:04:17.530 --> 00:04:20.170
the anthology and the search tool.

00:04:20.170 --> 00:04:23.370
Fortunoff Video Archive in the DHLab

00:04:23.370 --> 00:04:27.750
had started a partnership
beyond this project.

00:04:27.750 --> 00:04:31.320
This is our, sort of first
effort to work them together

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:34.280
by bringing Gabor Toth, the
post doctoral associate.

00:04:34.280 --> 00:04:37.550
We are funding the post
doctoral associate,

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:40.130
and the DHLab is
working together with him

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:41.370
to produce this project.

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:43.170
But in addition to this we're also looking

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:46.870
to bring in a developer to work

00:04:46.870 --> 00:04:50.350
on additional Fortunoff
projects in the future.

00:04:50.350 --> 00:04:51.573
- My name is Amanda Chemeche.

00:04:51.573 --> 00:04:55.010
I am a dual degree Masters
student at Yale University.

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:58.330
So I'm studying East Asian
studies and American studies.

00:04:58.330 --> 00:05:01.320
But both of them have an
interest in the history,

00:05:01.320 --> 00:05:05.785
the landscape and trauma that
takes place in the landscape,

00:05:05.785 --> 00:05:08.210
and investigating that
with digital media tools

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:11.630
such as Autocad, Rhino
and Adobe Illustrator.

00:05:11.630 --> 00:05:13.090
So, yah.

00:05:13.090 --> 00:05:16.240
It's actually based off of
Teju Cole's novel, Open City.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:19.720
And the protagonist is Julius and Julius

00:05:19.720 --> 00:05:23.090
is a Nigerian doctor
at Columbia University

00:05:23.090 --> 00:05:27.230
who traverses the city and
meditates on various events

00:05:27.230 --> 00:05:29.130
historically that have happened,

00:05:29.130 --> 00:05:30.700
and one of the main quotes of the book

00:05:30.700 --> 00:05:32.560
is that the site is a palimpsest,

00:05:32.560 --> 00:05:35.803
and a palimpsest is a
document where things

00:05:35.803 --> 00:05:38.090
have been written on it
and erased and re-written.

00:05:38.090 --> 00:05:41.240
You can find the imprint of those things

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:43.970
from the past in that document.

00:05:43.970 --> 00:05:46.930
And when you think about
atrocity and the development

00:05:46.930 --> 00:05:49.040
of Manhattan, like, you
know, wait a minute,

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:52.230
my fiance is an architecture student

00:05:52.230 --> 00:05:54.910
and we're using exploded
axons all the time,

00:05:54.910 --> 00:05:58.310
and what's a better
document, kind of, expressor,

00:05:58.310 --> 00:06:02.090
than an exploded axon to show
the palimpsest of history?

00:06:02.090 --> 00:06:05.800
So we picked four, four points
that Teju Cole talked about,

00:06:05.800 --> 00:06:09.600
which was pre-colonial
Manhattan when it was occupied

00:06:09.600 --> 00:06:11.233
by the Algonquin Lenape.

00:06:12.480 --> 00:06:15.370
Dutch colonial Manhattan, we picked 1664,

00:06:15.370 --> 00:06:18.660
right on the cusp of when
it was sold to the British.

00:06:18.660 --> 00:06:23.660
1863 draft riots and
2001, the 9-11 attacks.

00:06:24.150 --> 00:06:27.160
And we really wanted
to express two things.

00:06:27.160 --> 00:06:30.770
We wanted to talk about the
fact that he's articulating

00:06:30.770 --> 00:06:32.420
all this information,
but when you're talking

00:06:32.420 --> 00:06:34.670
about literature, you're cherry picking

00:06:34.670 --> 00:06:38.420
pieces of information from
within a broader archive,

00:06:38.420 --> 00:06:40.860
and we wanted to compare those two things.

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:44.470
And in order to do that, we
traced out his experiences,

00:06:44.470 --> 00:06:46.600
this is a programmatic axon.

00:06:46.600 --> 00:06:48.280
So that really shows these events

00:06:48.280 --> 00:06:51.670
layered on one another and
then separated by time.

00:06:51.670 --> 00:06:55.190
And at the same time, we
recreated from historic maps,

00:06:55.190 --> 00:06:59.310
these 3 dimensional interactive
spaces that have hot spots,

00:06:59.310 --> 00:07:01.760
and as you click on them,
you open up the archives.

00:07:01.760 --> 00:07:04.380
So I wanted people to understand
the wealth of information

00:07:04.380 --> 00:07:06.940
that he was tapping into and begin

00:07:06.940 --> 00:07:08.860
to actually explore these spaces.

00:07:08.860 --> 00:07:12.013
So it's kind of like time
travel in four distinct periods.

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:17.280
- What I'm presenting
here is a Python module.

00:07:17.280 --> 00:07:20.510
It's a software module
and it's designed for use

00:07:20.510 --> 00:07:24.590
inside of the Jupyter
Computational notebook format.

00:07:24.590 --> 00:07:28.690
And the idea is that
if you're a researcher

00:07:28.690 --> 00:07:30.830
who is working with a lot of image data,

00:07:30.830 --> 00:07:32.530
and in particular, if
you want to get, sort of,

00:07:32.530 --> 00:07:37.210
human eyes on the data,
there are some very, kind of,

00:07:37.210 --> 00:07:41.800
concise ways of producing
montages of images.

00:07:41.800 --> 00:07:43.750
Let me show you an example of this.

00:07:43.750 --> 00:07:46.870
Sort of these montages of
images and they can be sorted

00:07:46.870 --> 00:07:49.860
according to visual
properties of the images,

00:07:49.860 --> 00:07:53.660
or according to meta data
variables like place and time,

00:07:53.660 --> 00:07:55.700
and the idea is in particular,

00:07:55.700 --> 00:07:59.260
if you're a researcher in
the Humanistic Sciences

00:07:59.260 --> 00:08:03.250
and you want your human expertise

00:08:03.250 --> 00:08:05.550
to be the primary way of
apprehending these images,

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:08.800
rather than, sort of
like, machine perception,

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:11.490
this enables you to get
human eyes on your images

00:08:11.490 --> 00:08:14.000
in a fairly efficient way,
without having to leave

00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:16.490
the computational notebook format.

00:08:16.490 --> 00:08:20.080
One thing I've learned in
the process of producing

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:25.080
this software is I had to
try to strike a balance,

00:08:26.090 --> 00:08:29.753
in this case between the,
kind of, power of the tool,

00:08:31.160 --> 00:08:33.710
and what I mean by that
is the ability of the tool

00:08:33.710 --> 00:08:35.580
to do lots of different things,

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:39.000
and then for it to be
learnable by a fairly, kind of,

00:08:39.000 --> 00:08:40.190
sort of a new audience that is

00:08:40.190 --> 00:08:41.920
learning it for the first time.

00:08:41.920 --> 00:08:44.080
It can be a little bit
overwhelming if the tool has like,

00:08:44.080 --> 00:08:47.070
too many features, and so
you want to strike a balance

00:08:47.070 --> 00:08:49.970
between lots of features and
something being learnable

00:08:49.970 --> 00:08:53.080
and sort of, the user being
able to conceptualize it

00:08:53.080 --> 00:08:54.160
fairly easily in the beginning,

00:08:54.160 --> 00:08:56.580
and so I decided on having just

00:08:56.580 --> 00:08:58.710
four or five very basic plotting functions

00:09:00.453 --> 00:09:02.660
and that have a number of
options that you can change

00:09:02.660 --> 00:09:04.890
to adjust features of the plots

00:09:04.890 --> 00:09:06.740
and I found that to be
a fairly good balance.

00:09:06.740 --> 00:09:08.100
So there is a lot of power in there

00:09:08.100 --> 00:09:12.980
but the tool is organized
according to a fairly simple model

00:09:12.980 --> 00:09:15.320
that I think the user can,
kind of, learn right away.

00:09:15.320 --> 00:09:20.320
And so, this originally was
designed for the use case where,

00:09:20.390 --> 00:09:22.540
maybe you're already coding
in Python a little bit

00:09:22.540 --> 00:09:24.110
and you know a little bit of that stuff,

00:09:24.110 --> 00:09:25.800
but I think it could be an entry point

00:09:25.800 --> 00:09:27.590
for some people who want to learn that,

00:09:27.590 --> 00:09:28.940
but it's a little daunting and I think

00:09:28.940 --> 00:09:30.500
that is is kind of like a gentle entry

00:09:30.500 --> 00:09:33.253
into using Python and the notebook format.

00:09:34.620 --> 00:09:37.950
- This was collection of 300
or so tweets that we took.

00:09:37.950 --> 00:09:39.600
And what this is doing
is reading the tweets

00:09:39.600 --> 00:09:42.090
in chronological order, and
every time it comes across

00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:45.150
one of these key words,
it'll ping the bubble.

00:09:45.150 --> 00:09:46.740
And so the darker and larger the bubble,

00:09:46.740 --> 00:09:48.170
the more commonly it's seen,

00:09:48.170 --> 00:09:49.690
and it's really interesting
because you can watch

00:09:49.690 --> 00:09:51.370
the composition of
what's being talked about

00:09:51.370 --> 00:09:54.200
in the tweets as the
movement is going through.

00:09:54.200 --> 00:09:56.710
And a couple of interesting
things were Calhoun

00:09:56.710 --> 00:09:59.100
was initially not something
was brought up in the tweets.

00:09:59.100 --> 00:10:02.010
It was initially a lot of
master, a lot of slavery,

00:10:02.010 --> 00:10:04.310
a lot of civil rights, and
then as the movement went on,

00:10:04.310 --> 00:10:09.020
we see a lot of increased
attention to community, to campus,

00:10:09.020 --> 00:10:11.240
to principles, that kind of thing.

00:10:11.240 --> 00:10:13.030
So this was just an
interesting way to visualize

00:10:13.030 --> 00:10:15.530
what was happening in the
evolution of the tweets.

00:10:17.040 --> 00:10:19.990
This is the same set of tweets but instead

00:10:19.990 --> 00:10:23.030
what's happening is here is, sort of,

00:10:23.030 --> 00:10:24.370
the first tweets chronologically

00:10:24.370 --> 00:10:27.750
and then it goes around in this direction,

00:10:27.750 --> 00:10:28.610
and what you can do is you can

00:10:28.610 --> 00:10:30.880
sort of put your mouse
over top of the words

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:33.030
and it will map to where
they appear in the tweet.

00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:36.130
So you can take a look
at, for example, Yale.

00:10:36.130 --> 00:10:38.430
It's really prominent in
the first couple of tweets

00:10:38.430 --> 00:10:41.250
and in the later sections
it's not so prominent.

00:10:41.250 --> 00:10:44.160
For students, more prominent
in the later tweets

00:10:44.160 --> 00:10:46.133
than it is in the earlier
tweets, that kind of thing.

00:10:46.133 --> 00:10:48.290
It's just another way to
visualize what's happening

00:10:48.290 --> 00:10:49.790
in the tweets.

00:10:49.790 --> 00:10:51.100
- My name is Gina Hurley.

00:10:51.100 --> 00:10:53.830
I am an a Medieval Studies PhD candidate.

00:10:53.830 --> 00:10:56.620
- My name is Eric Ensley
and I am a PhD student

00:10:56.620 --> 00:10:58.620
in the English Department.

00:10:58.620 --> 00:11:01.610
- So we are presenting an
addition that was developed

00:11:01.610 --> 00:11:05.000
during a graduate student
workshop, the Digital Editing

00:11:05.000 --> 00:11:07.130
and the Manuscript Roll workshop,

00:11:07.130 --> 00:11:10.250
of which we've now hosted
several institutions.

00:11:10.250 --> 00:11:12.610
- Yes, and it's a two day workshop

00:11:12.610 --> 00:11:15.210
and we invite graduate
students and librarians,

00:11:15.210 --> 00:11:17.330
basically anybody studying
anything having to do

00:11:17.330 --> 00:11:19.980
with the Medieval period,
to come to our workshop

00:11:19.980 --> 00:11:23.020
and we train them to do XML and TEI.

00:11:23.020 --> 00:11:24.710
And so over the course of the workshop,

00:11:24.710 --> 00:11:27.060
they design and implement code

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:29.410
for a digital edition of a roll,

00:11:29.410 --> 00:11:32.530
and we are hosting that
on our website right now,

00:11:32.530 --> 00:11:35.900
and we are running through our
beta version of our platform.

00:11:35.900 --> 00:11:38.860
And we will have an archive of about,

00:11:38.860 --> 00:11:41.100
we have about 10 rolls
right now that we've done.

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:42.970
We will be hosting all those rolls online.

00:11:42.970 --> 00:11:44.920
So it's collaborative coding basically.

00:11:45.810 --> 00:11:49.600
- So Manuscript rolls are unusual objects,

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:52.020
and so trying to find a way to do justice

00:11:52.020 --> 00:11:55.860
to the actual physical
materiality of a manuscript roll,

00:11:55.860 --> 00:12:00.860
when most of our, most of the
existing mark up languages

00:12:00.950 --> 00:12:04.940
are thinking about codices,
of books, as opposed to this,

00:12:04.940 --> 00:12:07.670
you know, strange, long
object that you have to unroll

00:12:07.670 --> 00:12:10.780
and interact with in this
really interesting way.

00:12:10.780 --> 00:12:13.220
Thinking about a way that
our editions can do justice

00:12:13.220 --> 00:12:15.590
to the materiality of a
manuscript is something

00:12:15.590 --> 00:12:17.440
that we've been really interested in.

